[' Here's a Java method that addresses the given requirements:

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class HashUtil {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(HashUtil.class.getName());
    private static final int MAX_INPUT_LENGTH = 1024;

    public static String md5(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input cannot be null or empty.");
        }

        if (input.length() > MAX_INPUT_LENGTH) {
            input = input.substring(0, MAX_INPUT_LENGTH);
            LOGGER.warning("Input length exceeded the limit of " + MAX_INPUT_LENGTH + ". Truncated to " + input.length() + " characters.");
        }

        MessageDigest md;
        try {
            md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("MD5 algorithm not supported.", e);
        }

        md.update(input.getBytes());
        byte[] digest = md.digest();
        BigInteger bigInt = new BigInteger(1, digest);
        String hashedInput = bigInt.toString(16);

        while (hashedInput.length() < 32) {
            hashedInput = "0" + hashedInput;
        }

        return hashedInput;
    }
}
```

This method handles input validation, truncates long inputs to manage memory usage, uses the standard MessageDigest library for MD5 computation, and logs any errors or warnings. The time complexity of this method is O(n), where n is the length of the input string, as it performs a single pass through the input data during hashing.

To improve performance when dealing with large datasets, consider using parallel processing if multiple cores are available. However, be aware that this may introduce additional complexity and potential trade-offs in terms of memory usage and thread synchronization.

The method is designed to be easily replaceable for different hashing algorithms by modifying the MessageDigest instance creation line. To scale efficiently with increasing data volume, consider using a caching mechanism to store previously computed hashes and avoid unnecessary recomputations. This can help reduce the time complexity from O(n) to O(1) in many cases.

