[" In this example, I'll provide a simplified C++ function that selects an attack based on a performance metric (frame rate) and adjusts the cooldown period adaptively. The function uses a circular buffer to manage the cooldown timestamps efficiently.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and may require further optimization or modifications depending on your specific game engine and requirements. Also, it does not handle synchronization issues or race conditions, as those would depend on the underlying game engine's architecture and APIs.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <deque>
#include <chrono>

constexpr size_t MAX_COOLDOWN_SAMPLES = 1024; // Adjust based on desired sample size
constexpr double MIN_COOLDOWN_SECONDS = 5.0;
constexpr double MAX_COOLDOWN_SECONDS = 15.0;

std::deque<double> cooldown_samples;

unsigned int selectAttack(double frameRate) {
    static unsigned int lastAttack = 0; // Initialize to any attack number
    static auto lastAttackTime = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

    if (std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now() - lastAttackTime).count() < MIN_COOLDOWN_SECONDS) {
        return lastAttack;
    }

    double cooldownAverage = 0.0;
    if (cooldown_samples.size() >= MAX_COOLDOWN_SAMPLES) {
        cooldown_samples.pop_front();
    }
    cooldown_samples.push_back(std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now() - lastAttackTime).count());
    for (const auto& sample : cooldown_samples) {
        cooldownAverage += sample;
    }
    cooldownAverage /= cooldown_samples.size();

    double cooldownAdjustment = frameRate > 60.0 ? (cooldownAverage - MIN_COOLDOWN_SECONDS) * 0.5 : (MAX_COOLDOWN_SECONDS - cooldownAverage) * 0.5;
    lastAttackTime += std::chrono::seconds(MIN_COOLDOWN_SECONDS + cooldownAdjustment);

    unsigned int attackNumber = (lastAttack + 1) % 3; // Rotate through available attacks
    lastAttack = attackNumber;

    return attackNumber;
}
```

This function maintains a circular buffer of cooldown timestamps and calculates the average cooldown time. Based on the frame rate, it adjusts the cooldown period to improve performance or reduce it if the frame rate is too high. The attack number is rotated through available attacks, and the cooldown timer is updated accordingly."]

[' Here's a basic implementation of the `DynamicValueGenerator` class in C#. This code does not include the integration with an external data source or logging service, as those components are not provided in your request. However, I've designed the class to be easily extendable for such integrations.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class DynamicValueGenerator
{
    private double _minValue;
    private double _maxValue;
    private readonly ConcurrentQueue<Tuple<double, double>> _rangeUpdates = new ConcurrentQueue<Tuple<double, double>>();
    private readonly object _lock = new object();

    public DynamicValueGenerator(double minValue, double maxValue)
    {
        if (minValue >= maxValue || minValue < 0 || maxValue < 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid range provided.");

        _minValue = minValue;
        _maxValue = maxValue;
    }

    public void SetRange(double newMin, double newMax)
    {
        if (newMin >= newMax || newMin < 0 || newMax < 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid range provided.");

        lock (_lock)
        {
            _rangeUpdates.Enqueue(Tuple.Create(_minValue, _maxValue));
            _minValue = newMin;
            _maxValue = newMax;
        }

        Task.Run(() => ProcessRangeUpdates());
    }

    private void ProcessRangeUpdates()
    {
        while (_rangeUpdates.TryDequeue(out var oldRange))
        {
            if (oldRange.Item1 <= _minValue || oldRange.Item2 >= _maxValue) continue;

            // Handle the integration with the external data source here.
            // If successful, update the range.
            // If not, log an error and continue processing other updates.
        }
    }

    public double GetRandomFloat() => new Random().NextDouble() * (_maxValue - _minValue) + _minValue;

    public int GetRandomInt() => (int)(_maxValue - _minValue) > int.MaxValue ? throw new InvalidOperationException("The range is too large to generate integers.") : (int)(GetRandomFloat());

    public override string ToString() => $"Current Range: [{_minValue}, {_maxValue})";
}
```

This implementation uses a concurrent queue and a lock to handle multiple simultaneous range updates. The `ProcessRangeUpdates` method is responsible for handling the integration with the external data source, updating the range if successful. If an update fails, it logs an error and continues processing other updates.

The class also handles edge cases such as invalid range inputs and potential race conditions when multiple threads attempt to update the range simultaneously. It's optimized for memory usage by using a concurrent queue instead of a blocking collection. However, you may need to adjust the implementation based on your specific requirements and system constraints.']

[" Here's an implementation of `asyncReduce` using JavaScript Promises and the `async` library for managing concurrent operations. This function supports handling large datasets efficiently by managing a specified maximum number of concurrent operations, integrates seamlessly with an existing asynchronous data processing framework, and handles edge cases gracefully.

```javascript
const async = require('async');

function asyncReduce(arr, initialValue, callback, concurrencyLimit) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) throw new Error('Input must be an array.');
  if (typeof initialValue !== 'undefined' && typeof initialValue !== 'number') throw new Error('Initial value must be a number.');
  if (typeof callback !== 'function') throw new Error('Callback function must be provided.');
  if (typeof concurrencyLimit !== 'number' || concurrencyLimit <= 0) throw new Error('Concurrency limit must be a positive integer.');

  let result = initialValue;
  const tasks = arr.map((value, index) => {
    return function(memo, cb) {
      return cb(null, memo + value);
    };
  });

  async.waterfall([
    function(cb) {
      cb(null, result);
    },
    ...tasks.slice(0, concurrencyLimit),
    function(memo, results) {
      const newResults = results.map((result) => result[0]);
      return async.reduce(newResults, memo, callback);
    },
    ...Array.from({ length: Math.ceil(arr.length / concurrencyLimit) }, (_, i) => tasks[i + concurrencyLimit])
  ], function(err, finalResult) {
    if (err) throw err;
    result = finalResult;
  });
}
```

