[" Here's a JavaScript function named `processDOMAndString` that meets your requirements:

```javascript
async function processDOMAndString(str, sizeLimit, selector, config) {
  const results = [];
  let elements = document.querySelectorAll(selector);

  for (let i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
    const textContent = elements[i].textContent || '';
    if (textContent.length <= sizeLimit) {
      results.push(`${textContent}${str.length > textContent.length ? '/' : ''}`);
    } else {
      results.push(null);
    }

    if (config.apiEndpoint && !results[i]) {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(config.apiEndpoint, { timeout: config.timeout });
        const data = await response.json();

        if (data.styleRules) {
          applyStyles(elements[i], data.styleRules);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        console.error(`Error fetching API data: ${error}`);
        applyStyles(elements[i], config.fallbackStyle || {});
      }
    }
  }

  function applyStyles(element, styles) {
    for (const key in styles) {
      if (!styles.hasOwnProperty(key)) continue;
      element.style[key] = styles[key];
    }
  }

  return results;
}
```

This function first checks each matching DOM element's text content against the size limit. If it fits, it appends a forward slash to the string and adds it to the results array. If not, it adds null to the results array. The function also integrates external data from a third-party API endpoint if provided in the configuration object. It handles potential errors and network delays gracefully by using try-catch blocks for the API call. Additionally, it applies the received styling rules to the elements.

