[' Here's a C# implementation of the `DynamicRangeValueGenerator` class that meets your requirements:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Runtime.Serialization;

public class DynamicRangeValueGenerator
{
    private double minValue;
    private double maxValue;

    public DynamicRangeValueGenerator(double min, double max)
    {
        if (min >= max)
            throw new ArgumentException("Minimum value must be less than maximum value.");
        if (min < 0 || max < 0)
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("Both minimum and maximum values must be non-negative.");

        minValue = min;
        maxValue = max;
    }

    public void SetRange(double newMin, double newMax)
    {
        if (newMin >= newMax || newMin < minValue || newMax <= maxValue)
            throw new ArgumentException("New range must be valid and not overlap with the current range.");

        minValue = newMin;
        maxValue = newMax;
    }

    public double GetRandomFloat() => new Random().NextDouble() * (maxValue - minValue) + minValue;

    public int GetRandomInt() => (int)(GetRandomFloat() + 0.5);

    public override string ToString() => $"Current Range: [{minValue}, {maxValue})";

    public void ValidateAndAdjustRange(double newMin, double newMax, double adjustmentFactor)
    {
        if (adjustmentFactor <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("Adjustment factor must be positive.");

        SetRange(minValue + (newMin - minValue) * adjustmentFactor, maxValue + (newMax - maxValue) * adjustmentFactor);
    }

    public List<double> GetRandomFloats(int count) => Enumerable.Range(0, count).Select(i => GetRandomFloat()).ToList();

    public List<int> GetRandomInts(int count) => Enumerable.Range(0, count).Select(i => GetRandomInt()).ToList();
}
```

This implementation includes the requested features and handles edge cases such as invalid range inputs, negative min or max values, and overlapping ranges. It also supports batch generation of random floating-point numbers and integers. The `ValidateAndAdjustRange()` method allows users to adjust the range by a factor while ensuring that the new range remains valid and does not overlap with the previous range.

In a distributed system, you may want to consider implementing some form of consistency mechanism (e.g., using a centralized configuration service or a consensus algorithm) to ensure that different instances have consistent configurations. Additionally, you could implement a method for periodically checking and adjusting the generator's configuration based on the latest data from external sources.

To optimize performance in large-scale applications, consider using parallelism when generating batches of random values (e.g., by using `Parallel.ForEach()` instead of `Enumerable.Range()`). However, be aware that this may introduce additional complexity and potential issues related to thread safety.']

[' In this example, I'll provide a C++11 solution using std::unique_ptr for memory management, mutex for thread safety, and exception handling for graceful error handling. The code uses a custom `DataFeed` class to simulate external data feeds with potential discrepancies or delays.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <memory>

class DataFeed {
public:
    virtual std::string getString() = 0;
    virtual int getInt() = 0;
};

class BrokenDataFeed : public DataFeed {
public:
    std::string getString() override { throw std::runtime_error("Broken data feed"); }
    int getInt() override { return -1; }
};

struct LargeStruct {
    std::unique_ptr<char[]> str;
    int value;
};

std::mutex mtx;
std::vector<std::unique_ptr<LargeStruct>> generateData(int num, DataFeed& feed) {
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<LargeStruct>> data;

    std::default_random_engine generator;
    std::uniform_int_distribution<> distribution(1, 100);

    for (int i = 0; i < num; ++i) {
        std::string str = feed.getString();
        int value = feed.getInt();

        if (str.empty() || value == -1) {
            data.push_back(std::unique_ptr<LargeStruct>());
            continue;
        }

        size_t len = str.length();
        data.push_back(std::make_unique<LargeStruct>(new char[len + 1], value));
        std::copy(str.begin(), str.end(), data.back()->str.get());
        data.back()->str->back() = '\0';
    }

    return data;
}

std::vector<std::unique_ptr<LargeStruct>> generateDataConcurrently(int num, DataFeed& feed, int threads) {
    std::vector<std::thread> workers;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<LargeStruct>> data;

    auto chunkSize = num / threads;
    for (int i = 0; i < threads; ++i) {
        int start = i * chunkSize;
        int end = (i == threads - 1) ? num : start + chunkSize;
        workers.push_back(std::thread([&]() { data.insert(data.end(), generateData(end - start, feed).begin() + start, generateData(end - start, feed).end()); },));
    }

    for (auto& worker : workers) {
        worker.join();
    }

    return data;
}

void freeData(std::vector<std::unique_ptr<LargeStruct>>& data) {
    for (auto& structPtr : data) {
        if (structPtr) {
            delete[] structPtr->str.get();
            structPtr.reset();
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines three classes: `DataFeed`, `LargeStruct`, and a helper function `generateData()`. The `generateData()` function generates the required data structures with randomly generated integers and strings from an external data feed. If the data feed returns broken or malformed data, it skips that instance and continues with the next one.

The `generateDataConcurrently()` function generates data concurrently using multiple threads to improve performance and scalability. The `freeData()` function releases all dynamically allocated memory before exiting the function.']

