    if (!keys) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for keys\n");
        free(offsets);
        return NULL;
    }

    Record *records = (Record *)malloc(key_count * sizeof(Record));
    if (!records) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for records\n");
        free(offsets);
        free(keys);
        return NULL;
    }

    srand(time(NULL));

    // Generate unique offset values and store them in the offset array
    for (size_t i = 0; i < offset_count; ++i) {
        bool found = false;
        do {
            *offsets++ = rand() % 1000000 + 1; // Generate a random number between 1 and 1000000
            for (size_t j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                if (*(offsets - 1) == *(offsets - offset_count - j)) {
                    found = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        } while (found);
    }

    // Generate unique key values and store them in the keys array
    for (size_t i = 0; i < key_count; ++i) {
        bool found = false;
        do {
            *(keys + i) = rand() % 1000 + 1; // Generate a random number between 1 and 1000
            for (size_t j = 0; j < i; ++j) {
                if (*(keys + i) == *(keys + j)) {
                    found = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        } while (found);
    }

    // Initialize records with random values and store the corresponding offsets and keys
    for (size_t i = 0; i < key_count; ++i) {
        Record *record = &(records[i]);
        record->offset = offsets;
        record->key = keys + i;
        record->value = rand() % 2001 - 1000; // Generate a random number between -1000 and 1000
    }

    return records;
}
```

This function initializes the required memory for offsets, keys, and records. It generates unique offset and key values within specified ranges and stores them in the allocated arrays. Each record structure is initialized with a random value between -1000 and 1000, along with pointers to the corresponding offset and key. The function handles potential memory allocation failures and ensures that all allocated memory is properly managed to prevent leaks.']

[" Here's a basic implementation using JavaScript (ES6) with Node.js, Express, Redis, and async/await for handling asynchronous operations. This example assumes you have Redis installed and running.

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const redis = require('redis');
const axios = require('axios');
const app = express();
const client = redis.createClient();

let uniqueElements = new Set();
let processingTasks = [];

app.get('/api/data', async (req, res) => {
  const apiUrls = ['https://example-api1.com', 'https://example-api2.com']; // Replace with actual API URLs

  for (const url of apiUrls) {
    processingTasks.push(fetchData(url));
  }

  await Promise.all(processingTasks);

  res.json({ uniqueElements: Array.from(uniqueElements) });
});

async function fetchData(url) {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get(url);
    const data = response.data; // Assuming the API returns JSON data

    for (const item of data) {
      if (!uniqueElements.has(item)) {
        uniqueElements.add(item);
        client.set(item, '1', (err, reply) => {
          if (err) console.error(err);
        });
      }
    }
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(`Error fetching data from ${url}:`, error);
  }
}

// Handle pauses and resumes by preserving state across pauses
client.on('end', () => {
  console.log('Redis connection closed');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server is running on port 3000');
});
```

This example demonstrates a simple data processing pipeline that fetches data from multiple APIs, processes it (by tracking unique elements), and integrates with an external data processing service for batch processing using Redis. The code handles interruptions gracefully by preserving state across pauses.

To optimize for performance and reliability, consider implementing exponential backoff for API requests when encountering errors, rate limiting to avoid overloading APIs, and using a load balancer or circuit breaker pattern to handle varying service availability. Additionally, you can implement caching strategies to reduce the number of API calls based on the data's expiration time or frequency of updates.

For managing memory efficiently under varying load conditions, consider implementing a mechanism for evicting old elements from the Set when it reaches a certain size limit. This can be done by setting a maximum size and periodically iterating over the Set to remove elements that haven't been accessed recently (e.g., using Redis' TTL feature).

