[" I'm providing a simplified C++ example using the Standard Template Library (STL) that addresses some of the requirements you've mentioned. This solution uses a `std::vector` for efficient memory management and provides functions for adding, removing, and iterating over elements. The list is thread-safe with the help of `std::mutex`.

Please note that this example does not include the integration with an external data source or the random CRCFLAG setting, as those requirements would depend on specific details about your system's architecture and APIs.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <memory>
#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <atomic>

struct NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T {
    RX_UE_INFORMATION_TAG rx_ue_information_tag;
    int rnti;
    CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG crc_indication_rel8_tag;
};

class DynamicList {
private:
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T>> data;
    std::mutex mtx;
    std::atomic<int> size{0};

public:
    void add(const NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T& element) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        data.emplace_back(new NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T{element});
        ++size;
    }

    bool remove(int rnti) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        auto it = std::find_if(data.begin(), data.end(), [rnti](const auto& element) { return element->rnti == rnti; });
        if (it != data.end()) {
            data.erase(it);
            --size;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    std::vector<const NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T*>::reverse_iterator begin() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        return data.rbegin();
    }

    std::vector<const NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T*>::reverse_iterator end() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        return data.rend();
    }
};
```

This example demonstrates a basic implementation of the requirements you've mentioned. However, it is essential to consider your specific use case and adjust the code accordingly for optimal performance and scalability. Additionally, you may need to implement a garbage collection mechanism to clean up unused resources periodically."]

