const unsigned int HASH_SIZE = 1024;
unsigned int active_records = 0;
Record *records = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t records_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

unsigned int custom_hash(int key) {
    unsigned int hash = 5381;
    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(key) * CHAR_BIT; ++i) {
        hash = ((hash << 5) + hash) ^ key >> (sizeof(key) * CHAR_BIT - i);
    }
    return hash % HASH_SIZE;
}

void init_list(unsigned int n) {
    if (n <= active_records) return;

    Record *new_records = realloc(records, sizeof(Record) * n);
    if (!new_records) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Out of memory\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    records = new_records;
}

void insert(int key) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&records_lock);
    init_list(HASH_SIZE);

    unsigned int index = custom_hash(key);
    for (unsigned int i = index; i < HASH_SIZE; ++i) {
        if (!records[i].value) {
            records[i].value = key;
            active_records++;
            break;
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&records_lock);
}

int retrieve(int key) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&records_lock);
    unsigned int index = custom_hash(key);
    for (unsigned int i = index; i < HASH_SIZE; ++i) {
        if (records[i].value == key) {
            break;
        }
    }

    Record result;
    memcpy(&result, &records[index], sizeof(Record));
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&records_lock);

    return result.value;
}
```

This implementation uses a dynamic array to store the records and a custom hashing function to distribute keys evenly across the array. Concurrent access is managed using a locking strategy, with a single global lock protecting the entire list. The allocation strategy used here is fixed-size blocks for each record, which helps minimize fragmentation.

Error handling is limited in this example, but it includes checking for out-of-memory conditions and invalid input parameters. The function can be optimized further by aligning data structures to cache lines or using prefetching techniques as needed.

To adapt to changes in system requirements, you may need to adjust the `HASH_SIZE` parameter or implement more advanced collision resolution strategies.']

