[" Here's a basic implementation of the described task using Promises, Map for caching, and Fetch API for loading files. This solution is modular, scalable, and handles asynchronous loading, execution sequence, error handling, caching, and integration with an external API.

```javascript
class Loader {
  constructor(apiUrl) {
    this.cache = new Map();
    this.apiUrl = apiUrl;
  }

  async load(url) {
    if (this.cache.has(url)) return this.cache.get(url);

    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      const contentType = response.headers.get('content-type');
      const script = document.createElement('script');
      script.src = url;
      script.type = contentType || 'application/javascript';

      return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        script.onload = () => resolve(script);
        script.onerror = (event) => reject(new Error(`Failed to load ${url}: ${event.message}`));
        document.head.appendChild(script);
      });
    } catch (error) {
      return Promise.reject(new Error(`Failed to fetch ${url}: ${error.message}`));
    }
  }

  async execute(dependencies, mainScriptUrl) {
    const loadedFiles = [];

    for (const url of dependencies) {
      const loadedFile = await this.load(url);
      loadedFiles.push(loadedFile);
      this.cache.set(url, loadedFile);
    }

    const mainScript = await this.load(mainScriptUrl);
    loadedFiles.push(mainScript);
    this.cache.set(mainScriptUrl, mainScript);

    return Promise.all(loadedFiles);
  }

  async updateDependencies(mainScriptUrl) {
    const metadata = await fetch(`${this.apiUrl}/metadata/${mainScriptUrl}`).then(response => response.json());
    return this.execute(metadata.dependencies, mainScriptUrl);
  }
}
```

This Loader class can be used as follows:

```javascript
const loader = new Loader('https://api.example.com/loader');
loader.execute(['file1.js', 'file2.js'], 'main.js')
  .then(() => console.log('Scripts loaded and executed successfully.'))
  .catch((error) => console.error(error));
```

The trade-offs between the complexity of the caching logic and performance benefits are as follows:

1. Complexity: The caching mechanism adds some complexity to the code, but it significantly reduces redundant network requests, improving overall performance, especially when dealing with a large number of files.

2. Performance: Caching minimizes latency by reusing previously loaded files within the same session, making the system more responsive and efficient. However, caching does require additional memory to store the loaded files, which may impact performance on devices with limited resources.

3. Scalability: The modular design of the Loader class allows for easy addition of new files and dependencies, ensuring that the solution remains scalable as the project grows in size and complexity.

4. Maintenance: The caching mechanism simplifies maintenance by reducing the number of network requests required during development and testing, making it easier to iterate on the codebase without encountering frequent errors due to network issues.

Overall, the benefits of implementing a caching mechanism outweigh the added complexity, as it improves performance, scalability, and maintainability while minimizing redundant network requests."]

