[' Here's a simplified C++ implementation using the Standard Template Library (STL) for the dynamic work queue, with some key components:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <chrono>
#include <atomic>
#include <memory>
#include <boost/log/trivial.hpp>

struct Operation {
    int id;
    int priority;
    std::function<void()> callback;
};

struct Record {
    std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point timestamp;
    bool status;
};

class WorkQueue {
public:
    WorkQueue(size_t capacity)
        : offsets_(capacity), keys_(capacity), records_(capacity),
          priority_queue_(std::greater<Operation>()), log_(BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(info)) {}

    void enqueue(int key, int priority, std::function<void()> callback) {
        auto op = std::make_unique<Operation>(key, priority, std::move(callback));
        priority_queue_.push(*op);
        ops_.emplace(op->id, std::move(op));
    }

    void dequeue() {
        if (!priority_queue_.empty()) {
            auto op = priority_queue_.top();
            priority_queue_.pop();
            ops_.erase(op->id);
            // Perform data integrity checks here
            // ...
        }
    }

    void sweep() {
        std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        for (auto it = ops_.begin(); it != ops_.end();) {
            auto op = it->second.get();
            if ((now - op->timestamp.time_since_epoch()) > timeout_) {
                // Log expired operation and remove from priority queue and map
                log_(BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(warning)) << "Expired operation: id=" << op->id;
                it = ops_.erase(it);
            } else {
                ++it;
            }
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<long> offsets_;
    std::vector<int> keys_;
    std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Record>> records_;
    std::priority_queue<Operation> priority_queue_;
    std::unordered_map<int, std::unique_ptr<Operation>> ops_;
    std::chrono::seconds timeout_{10}; // Configurable timeout (10 seconds by default)
    boost::log::trivial::logger log_;
};
```

This implementation uses a priority queue to manage the operations based on their priority level. The `WorkQueue` class handles enqueuing, dequeuing, and sweeping expired operations. It also includes an external logging framework for tracking allocation failures and resource leaks.

Memory usage is optimized by using smart pointers (unique_ptr) to manage the memory of the Record and Operation objects. However, this implementation does not explicitly use memory pools due to its simplicity.

The system is designed to be resilient to failures by logging expired operations and removing them from the priority queue and map during the sweep operation. In case of a partial system crash, the system can recover gracefully by reinitializing the WorkQueue object and re-enqueuing the pending operations.

Trade-offs between performance, memory usage, and system resilience include:

1. Performance: Using a priority queue for managing operations improves performance when handling high-priority tasks, but may cause slower dequeue times for low-priority tasks due to the nature of priority queues.
2. Memory Usage: The use of smart pointers helps minimize memory fragmentation and potential leaks, but may increase memory usage slightly due to the overhead of managing the pointers.
3. System Resilience: Logging expired operations provides a mechanism for detecting and recovering from failures, but may impact performance due to logging overhead. Additionally, the system's resilience depends on the chosen external logging framework's reliability and recovery mechanisms.']

