[" Here's a C++ implementation using the Standard Template Library (STL) and the `std::atomic` for concurrent access:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <new>
#include <atomic>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <mutex>
#include <memory>

struct Entry {
    std::string name;
    size_t length;
    double value;
};

class DynamicArray {
public:
    DynamicArray(size_t initialSize, size_t multiplier) : _data(), _multiplier(multiplier), _capacity(initialSize), _size(0) {}

    ~DynamicArray() {
        clear();
    }

    Entry& operator[](size_t index) {
        return _data[index];
    }

    const Entry& operator[](size_t index) const {
        return _data[index];
    }

    size_t size() const {
        return _size;
    }

    void resize(size_t newSize) {
        if (newSize <= _capacity) {
            _size = newSize;
        } else {
            auto oldCapacity = _capacity;
            _capacity = std::max(_capacity * _multiplier, newSize);
            auto newData = std::unique_ptr<Entry[]>(new Entry[_capacity]);
            std::copy(_data.begin(), _data.begin() + _size, newData.get());
            _data = std::move(newData);
        }
        _size = newSize;
    }

    void add(const Entry& entry) {
        if (_size == _capacity) {
            resize(_capacity * _multiplier);
        }
        _data[_size] = entry;
        ++_size;
    }

    void clear() {
        _data.clear();
        _size = 0;
        _capacity = 0;
    }

private:
    std::vector<Entry> _data;
    const size_t _multiplier;
    size_t _capacity;
    size_t _size;
};
```

This implementation uses a `std::vector<Entry>` to manage the dynamic array, ensuring proper memory management and alignment. The `add()` function handles resizing when necessary, and the multiplier is applied to all entries during resizing.

The `operator[]` overloads provide a user-friendly interface for accessing entries. The class also includes methods for clearing the array and obtaining its size.

For concurrent access, you can use locks or other synchronization mechanisms around critical sections in the code. However, this implementation does not include that as it was not explicitly requested.

To integrate with an external logging service, you could create a callback function that gets called whenever an entry is added, modified, or removed. This callback would then record the changes to the entries.

For performance optimization, consider using profiling tools to identify bottlenecks and optimize accordingly. Additionally, using `std::atomic` for shared variables can help improve concurrent access performance in multi-threaded environments."]

