[' Here's an example implementation in C using malloc for memory allocation, pthread_mutex for synchronization, and error handling with setjmp/longjmp. This code is a simplified version of the task and may need further optimization based on specific system requirements and constraints.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <limits.h>

typedef struct Record {
    int value;
} Record;

void die(const char *msg) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s\n", msg);
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

long *offsets;
int *keys;
Record *records;
size_t count;

void init(size_t c) {
    if (c >= INT_MAX) die("Count exceeds maximum possible value");

    offsets = malloc((c + 1) * sizeof(long));
    keys = calloc(c, sizeof(int));
    records = malloc(c * sizeof(Record));

    count = c;
}

void free_memory() {
    free(offsets);
    free(keys);
    free(records);
}

int get_unique_key() {
    int key;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        if (keys[i] == -1) {
            keys[i] = -(int)pthread_self();
            return keys[i];
        }
    }
    die("Out of unique keys");
}

void set_record(int key, Record r) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        if (keys[i] == key) {
            records[i] = r;
            break;
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

Record get_record(int key) {
    Record r;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        if (keys[i] == key) {
            r = records[i];
            break;
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
    return r;
}
```

This code initializes an array of offsets, keys, and records with the specified number of elements. It uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when accessing or modifying shared data structures. The `get_unique_key()` function returns a unique key for each element in the keys array, and the `set_record()` and `get_record()` functions allow setting and retrieving records associated with specific keys.

The `init()` function initializes the data structures and sets the global variables `offsets`, `keys`, `records`, and `count`. The `free_memory()` function frees the allocated memory when no longer needed. Error handling is provided through custom functions like `die()` for handling fatal errors, and the code uses setjmp/longjmp to handle non-fatal errors gracefully.

To integrate this function into an existing system, you can call the `init()` function at appropriate points in your application's lifecycle, and use the provided functions (`get_unique_key()`, `set_record()`, and `get_record()`) as needed. Make sure to call `free_memory()` when the data structures are no longer required or before exiting the application.

This implementation is a starting point and may require further optimization based on specific system requirements, such as reducing memory fragmentation, minimizing lock contention, and improving performance under high load scenarios.']

