[' I'm providing a simplified version of the requested function in C++ using the std::unordered_map for efficient lookup and std::mutex for thread safety. This implementation does not include all the optional features you mentioned but serves as a foundation that can be extended further.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <memory>
#include <stdexcept>

struct Connection {
    int id;
    enum class State { IDLE, ESTABLISHED, CLOSED } state;
    std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::steady_clock> establishment_time;
    std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::steady_clock> last_activity_time;
    int error_count = 0;

    Connection(int id) : id(id), state(State::IDLE), establishment_time(std::chrono::steady_clock::now()) {}
};

class ConnectionManager {
private:
    std::unordered_map<int, std::unique_ptr<Connection>> connections;
    std::mutex mutex;
    int max_inactivity_seconds = 60;
    double max_error_rate = 0.1;

public:
    ConnectionManager(int max_inactivity_seconds, double max_error_rate) : max_inactivity_seconds(max_inactivity_seconds), max_error_rate(max_error_rate) {}

    Connection* establish_connection(int id) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        auto it = connections.find(id);
        if (it != connections.end()) throw std::invalid_argument("Connection ID already exists");

        auto conn = std::make_unique<Connection>(id);
        connections[id] = std::move(conn);
        log_connection_establishment(conn.get());
        return conn.get();
    }

    void close_connection(Connection* conn) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        auto it = connections.find(conn->id);
        if (it == connections.end()) throw std::runtime_error("Invalid connection");

        it->second->state = Connection::State::CLOSED;
        log_connection_close(conn);
    }

    Connection* query_connection(int id) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        auto it = connections.find(id);
        if (it == connections.end()) return nullptr;
        it->second->last_activity_time = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        return it->second.get();
    }

    void monitor() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex);
        for (auto& [id, conn] : connections) {
            auto elapsed = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::seconds>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - conn->last_activity_time).count();
            if (elapsed > max_inactivity_seconds || conn->error_count > max_error_rate * connections.size()) {
                close_connection(conn.get());
            }
        }
    }
};
```

This code defines a `Connection` struct and a `ConnectionManager` class that manages the dynamic collection of network connections. The `ConnectionManager` class provides operations to establish, close, and query connections, as well as a monitor function for automatically closing unhealthy connections based on user-defined thresholds for inactivity and error rates.

The code uses std::unordered_map for efficient lookup and std::mutex for thread safety. The `establish_connection`, `close_connection`, and `query_connection` functions handle concurrent access from multiple threads, ensuring thread safety without introducing performance bottlenecks. The monitor function is designed to run periodically to check the health of connections.

The code does not include the logging system interaction as it was not provided in the question. You can easily integrate it by calling the `log_connection_establishment` and `log_connection_close` functions when necessary. The implementation also does not include the connection pool feature, but you can extend it by managing a pool of pre-allocated connections instead of creating new ones every time.']

