[" I'll provide a simplified C++ example using std::vector for dynamic memory management and std::atomic for thread safety. This code doesn't include error handling or potential future extensions but serves as a good starting point.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <new>
#include <atomic>
#include <type_traits>

struct Task {
    int id;
    enum class Status : std::uint8_t { PENDING, IN_PROGRESS, COMPLETED };
    Status status = Status::PENDING;
    double duration = 0.0;
};

class TaskManager {
public:
    explicit TaskManager(size_t maxTasks, size_t batchSize)
        : _maxTasks(maxTasks), _batchSize(batchSize), _tasks(), _nextIndex(_tasks.size()) {}

    Task& addTask() {
        if (_nextIndex >= _tasks.size()) {
            allocateMore();
        }
        auto& task = _tasks[_nextIndex++];
        new (&task) Task(); // Construct in-place to avoid memory allocation overhead
        return task;
    }

    const std::vector<Task>& getTasks() const { return _tasks; }

private:
    void allocateMore() {
        size_t newSize = _nextIndex + _batchSize;
        _tasks.resize(newSize);
    }

    std::atomic<size_t> _nextIndex;
    std::vector<Task, std::allocator<Task>> _tasks;
    const size_t _maxTasks;
    const size_t _batchSize;
};
```

This code creates a `TaskManager` class that manages a dynamic collection of `Task` objects. The constructor takes the maximum number of tasks and an initial batch size for allocation as parameters. The `addTask()` function adds a new task to the collection, dynamically allocating more memory when necessary in increments defined by the batch size.

The `Task` struct contains the required fields (id, status, duration) and uses an enumeration type for the status field. The `TaskManager` class uses std::vector for dynamic memory management and std::atomic to ensure thread safety. The tasks are constructed in-place to minimize memory allocation overhead.

The provided code doesn't include error handling or potential future extensions, but it serves as a good starting point for further development."]

